The "From a past year" mode uses the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) [Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U), series CUUR0000SA0](https://data.bls.gov/timeseries/CUUR0000SA0), which covers all items for the U.S. city average and is not seasonally adjusted. To find today's value, the calculator multiplies your amount by the CPI-U for September 2026, the latest month in the data (336.94), and divides by the CPI-U annual average for the year you choose. For $10,000 in 2010, it divides by that year's annual average of 218.056. The result is $15,452 in September 2026 dollars, with prices up 54.5%. The calculator treats an annual average as mid-year, so that gap spans about 16.2 years and works out to about 2.7% a year on average. In our internal calculation, we checked this example in two separate programs, and you can enter $10,000 and 2010 above to see the same numbers.